WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.610
GitHub is a website that makes it easy

00:00:02.610 --> 00:00:05.085
to share an entire Git repository with other people.

00:00:05.085 --> 00:00:08.595
For example, here's a repository that we've created at Udacity.

00:00:08.595 --> 00:00:13.335
This repository is the source for a browser-based create your own adventure story.

00:00:13.335 --> 00:00:15.555
Here are all the files in the repository.

00:00:15.555 --> 00:00:18.895
I can click around and navigate the directory structure.

00:00:18.895 --> 00:00:20.730
It can look at the commit history.

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:24.135
If I go back to the main page and scroll down a little bit,

00:00:24.135 --> 00:00:25.965
I can see some information about the project.

00:00:25.965 --> 00:00:28.170
I can also directly view the contents of files,

00:00:28.170 --> 00:00:31.395
assuming their plain text like code or the story.

00:00:31.395 --> 00:00:34.980
GitHub also supports a couple of other formats for things like images,

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:36.855
3D models, and map data.

00:00:36.855 --> 00:00:41.825
In this file, I can click through to the story itself and start making some choices.

00:00:41.825 --> 00:00:45.545
You can always go back to the main page of the repository by clicking here.

00:00:45.545 --> 00:00:47.195
This is a public repository.

00:00:47.195 --> 00:00:50.170
So anybody can look at these contents and suggest changes.

00:00:50.170 --> 00:00:52.190
Caroline and I will go over how this works in

00:00:52.190 --> 00:00:54.770
detail in this lesson and by the end of the lesson,

00:00:54.770 --> 00:00:56.570
you'll be able to contribute to it yourself.

00:00:56.570 --> 00:00:58.025
This is a living project.

00:00:58.025 --> 00:00:59.600
By the time you visit this page,

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:01.160
things might look a little bit different,

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:03.085
but the basics will be the same.

00:01:03.085 --> 00:01:06.605
GitHub is the home of many public projects like this one.

00:01:06.605 --> 00:01:08.765
A lot of public projects are open source,

00:01:08.765 --> 00:01:12.200
meaning that you can copy and modify the code for your own purposes.

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:16.805
GitHub also lets you suggest changes and send them back to the creator of the project.

00:01:16.805 --> 00:01:21.325
Some of these open source projects are pretty small and relatively unknown.

00:01:21.325 --> 00:01:24.315
Like MathQuill, which only has 10 contributors.

00:01:24.315 --> 00:01:25.770
You might recognize this one.

00:01:25.770 --> 00:01:27.650
Some projects are much larger,

00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:31.060
like iPython, an interactive shell for Python.

00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:33.395
It's nicer than the one you get by default.

00:01:33.395 --> 00:01:35.929
Another large project is Bootstrap,

00:01:35.929 --> 00:01:38.935
which is a framework that makes it easier to do web development.

00:01:38.935 --> 00:01:44.315
There's also jQuery, a JavaScript library that makes it easy to interact with HTML,

00:01:44.315 --> 00:01:47.480
an atom, a text editor designed for editing

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:50.545
code and allowing users to customize their experience.

00:01:50.545 --> 00:01:53.660
These are projects that anyone could theoretically contribute to.

00:01:53.660 --> 00:01:57.995
Of course, any changes must be accepted by the people maintaining the project.

00:01:57.995 --> 00:02:01.550
But even if the original author chooses not to incorporate your changes,

00:02:01.550 --> 00:02:03.595
you can still keep your own version.

00:02:03.595 --> 00:02:07.130
GitHub aims to promote open source by allowing anyone to host

00:02:07.130 --> 00:02:12.090
public repositories for free and charging for private repositories.

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:14.300
Many companies, including Udacity,

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:18.830
use Git to keep track of changes to their proprietary code and use GitHub to host

00:02:18.830 --> 00:02:21.260
a private copy of their repository and allow

00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:24.280
for easy code reviews when developers submit changes.

00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:27.335
For this lesson, you'll be interacting with GitHub a lot,

00:02:27.335 --> 00:02:31.325
creating, modifying, and suggesting changes to public repositories.

00:02:31.325 --> 00:02:33.175
You'll need to set up an account.

00:02:33.175 --> 00:02:34.850
This is pretty straightforward,

00:02:34.850 --> 00:02:36.800
but you'll probably also want to get setup to

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:39.155
smoothly interact with GitHub from the command line,

00:02:39.155 --> 00:02:41.120
which is a little bit more involved.

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:45.360
You'll see instructions for how to get everything set up after this video.

